this is two more Hussein standing in for  freelance reviewer Richard weakling  the dark Sider series has always worn  its inspirations on its sleeve but at  least there was a sense of focus and  consistent design omnipresent in all of  its moving parts those first two games  may have been derivative but they took  concepts and built on them in fun and  engaging ways that elevated their strong  points Darksiders 3 is the antithesis of  this approach feeling muddled and  unfocused with an uneven design that  trickles down and negatively affects  each of its disparate systems what  pathetic Satan he's brought upon  yourself after both war and death had  their fun in Darksiders three's  predecessors it's now the turn of the  perpetually angry fury fury wills a  bladed whip known as the Bob's of scorn  which offers both decent range and a  satisfying feedback of meaty hits once  you're up close and personal in an  enemy's face throughout the game you'll  acquire hollows that grant unique  secondary weapons and they open up your  traversal options with different  elemental effects  combos are relatively easy to execute  with one button dedicated to primary  attacks and another to those  aforementioned secondary strikes button  mashing is enough to get you through  most encounters but mixing in slight  delays between button presses will allow  you to pull off air combos and other  similarly stylish moves it certainly  looks the part of a flamboyant action  game but these flashy combos are only  really feasible against weaker enemies  dockside history has taken some clear  inspiration from Dark Souls so Furies  low survivability forces you to approach  combat in a way that belies its  exuberant combos enemies are fast and  hit hard and are regularly found in  groups with no stamina meter to speak of  there's an emphasis on dodging and  keeping out of danger that does deviate  from Dark Souls a stringent use of  energy management each perfectly timed  dodge is rewarded with a slow-motion  flourish and the chance to count with a  powerful arcane attack and most clashes  are built around Fury's ability to weave  out of the way of incoming sword slashes  and ravenous claws there's a good  variety of enemy types to reading their  attack patterns and knowing when to  evade is paramount to defeating almost  every enemy you can't simply banish with  a single combo but Darksiders 3 never  leans into this considered approach to  combat enough and out of place vestiges  of its flashy counterpoint regularly  caused frustration as it seems to split  between wanting to be two very different  types of games you may have air juggles  and various strings of deadly moves at  your disposal but you're often forced to  settle for safe combos because anything  else would leave you wide open to a  devastating attack then there's the way  you'd lock on to enemies this was  clearly designed with one-on-one battles  in mind and certainly doesn't fit in a  game as challenging as this while there  are undoubtedly some tough enemies in  Darksiders free the real difficulty  comes from facing more than one enemy at  a time rather than a singular threat  groups of enemies are intrinsically  tougher due to their numbers and the  unwieldy lock on and a terrible camera  make these fights a lot more challenging  than they otherwise would be the camera  has a tendency to block your view with  walls and any object in the vicinity  which is only exacerbated by the clocks  Truffaut begin vironment that dominate  the majority of the game dying because  you can't see or because switching  targets is too fiddly our constant  annoyance not to mention the number of  times you're hit by an attack from off  screen there's an indicator for incoming  attacks but it's incredibly difficult to  discern in the thick of action and no  such warning exist for projectiles the  oxide is free also strips out a lot of  the RPG elements from its immediate  predecessor killing enemies rewards you  with souls which can also be found  throughout the game world in consumable  clusters each time you reach a  checkpoint you can trade them to a  demonic merchant in order to level up  three attributes health strength and  arcane it's a very simplistic  progression system and while you can  lose souls by dying and must then  retrieve them again there's never any  tension born from the threat of  perishing and losing them all because  you can Bank Souls even if you don't  have enough to level them up weapons can  be upgraded to increase their damage  output and enhancements will augment  your arsenal with buffs but there's no  sense of individuality here and combat  never evolves because all you're doing  is boosting your damage output this is a  problem when combat is all-encompassing  there are some rudimentary puzzles  sprinkled throughout but they're very  few and far in between none of this is  particularly engaging and that goes for  the rare instance of platforming as well  grabbing onto ledges is too  temperamental and docksiders really  lacks a cohesive visual language that  makes some platforming sections more  convoluted than they should be the  apocalyptic wasteland of Earth just  isn't that interesting to traverse  either the interconnected wall is made  up of dilapidated office buildings grimy  subways and flooded industrial areas  each of these locales is enveloped by  muted colors dominated by beige and gray  with only a couple of areas deviating  from this bland design your quest might  revolve around tracking down and killing  the seven deadly sins but the  environments you're in rarely reflect  their diverse personalities which feels  like a squandered opportunity  verticality plays a substantial role in  these environments but there's no sense  of scale when you're regularly confined  to dank corridors in Subway's and caves  it's a shame too because while the bulky  comic book art style of Joe Madureira is  still reflected in the excellent  character design the backdrop for these  larger-than-life beings is this generic  insipid world though I don't want to  humanity is tragically overrated I am  NOT here for them I'm docksiders three  is also rife with constant framerate  issues even on a ps4 Pro on top of  crashes sound glitches and other  technical misgivings - the oxide is free  retrogrades on its predecessors with an  unfocused approach that constantly  clashes with itself there are remnants  of a good game here buried within the  vivacious combos of a combat style this  game doesn't want to embrace  unfortunately it's buried far too deep  to ever Salvage cannot believe I am  guilty of these crimes something is  wrong with the you  be vigilant fury  bosses conspire against us heed my words
 

